<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Adamawa Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1809 CE – 1901 CE · capital Yola</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest emirate of the Sokoto Caliphate, stretching from the Benue into what is now Cameroon, and split by Britain and Germany in 1901.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibbo-adama">Modibbo Adama</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar, received a flag from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, traditionally in 1806 or 1809, to spread the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in Fombina, the southern lands. His followers conquered or absorbed many local chiefdoms on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> highlands, and in 1841 Adama founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> as the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was a loose federation of dozens of sub-emirates, or lamidates, such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lamidate-of-ngaoundere">Ngaoundere</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garoua">Garoua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banyo">Banyo</a>, each ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> lamido who paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a>, which in turn paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">Slave raiding</a> against non-Muslim peoples supplied tribute and labour, and the region was among the largest slave-holding societies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Lawal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibbo-adama">Adama</a>'s son, ruled from 1847 to 1872.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1901 British forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Zubeiru fled; the Anglo-German border gave most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a>. The British part became Adamawa Province in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>, and the Lamido of Adamawa still reigns at Yola.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/adamawa-emirate.jpg" alt="Adamawa Emirate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: 7bdIa9 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adamawa_State_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1809 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yola</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Modibbo Adama, Lawal, Zubeiru</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Fulfulde</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fombina, Emirate of Adamawa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1855&amp;focus=adamawa-emirate" aria-label="Adamawa Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5025 1834 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Adamawa Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5345 2090L5144 1947M5345 2090L5170 2068M5345 2090L5256 2278"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5144 1947h0M5170 2068h0M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5345 2090h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5345 2090h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yola. <a href="/atlas?year=1855&amp;focus=adamawa-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

